course: biology
lesson: 339
title: Park trees: telling oaks, maples, chestnuts, birches, limes and willows apart
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Trees in a park are like people in a crowd: once you know a few clues — the shape of the face, the clothes, the voice — you can tell them apart even from far away.
explain: For trees the clues are leaves, seeds, bark and buds. The oak drops acorns in little cups, the maple's leaf is shaped like an open hand and its seeds spin down like helicopters, and the horse chestnut's leaf spreads out like a hand with five to seven fingers.
explain: Park a car under a lime tree in July and come back to a windscreen covered in sticky drops: the aphids up in the leaves have been dribbling sweet honeydew all afternoon. Hug a tree with your arms and a tape measure and you can even guess its age.
explain: In cartoons every tree is a green lollipop. Real trees each have a face of their own, and in winter, when the leaves are gone, bark, buds and shape still give them away.
fact: Oak leaves have wavy edges and acorns sit in little cups; an oak can live for many hundreds of years, and one big oak can be home to hundreds of kinds of insects.
fact: Maple leaves are shaped like a hand, usually with five points, maple seeds spin like helicopters as they fall, and the sycamore in Britain is a kind of maple.
fact: A horse chestnut leaf is made of five to seven leaflets like fingers, and a fallen leaf leaves a horseshoe-shaped scar on the twig with dots like the nails in a horseshoe.
fact: A sweet chestnut has long toothed leaves and bark that spirals around the trunk; its nuts can be roasted and eaten, and its case is much spikier than a horse chestnut's.
fact: Birches have white bark and small triangular leaves, are often the first trees to grow on open land, and in Ukraine and other countries people drink birch sap in spring.
fact: Lime trees have heart-shaped leaves; aphids on them drop sweet honeydew, their summer flowers smell sweet and bees love them, and they are not related to the lime fruit.
fact: Willows love wet ground and grow fast; willow bark has a chemical used long ago to ease pain, and the medicine aspirin was later made from a similar chemical.
fact: Willow catkins are flowers, soft like a cat's paw, which is why people call them pussy willow, and they are early food for bees.
fact: Each ring in a tree stump is one year of growth; wide rings show good years, thin rings show dry or cold years, and the oldest trees have thousands of rings.
fact: Many trees grow about 2.5 centimetres around the trunk each year, so a trunk two metres around may be about 80 years old, but this is only a rough guess because trees grow at different speeds.
fact: In winter, bark, buds and shape still tell trees apart: ash has black buds, beech has long pointed buds, birch bark is white and oak bark is rough and cracked.
exercise: How can you recognise an oak? | Wavy-edged leaves and acorns sitting in little cups | Leaves shaped like a hand, with seeds that spin down like helicopters | White bark that peels | Heart-shaped leaves that drip honeydew
exercise: How long can an oak tree live? | Many hundreds of years | About ten years | About fifty years at most | Only until it drops its first acorns
exercise: Why is an old oak good for wildlife? | One big oak can be home to hundreds of kinds of insects | Animals can eat its soft, sweet bark all through the year | Oaks are poisonous to insects | Oaks never lose their leaves
exercise: How many points does a maple leaf usually have? | Five | Two | Ten | Twenty
exercise: How do maple seeds fall? | They spin like helicopters | They drop straight down like stones | They float on fluffy parachutes | They burst out of a spiky case
exercise: Which tree in Britain is a kind of maple? | The sycamore | The oak | The birch | The willow
exercise: How many leaflets make up a horse chestnut leaf? | Five to seven, like fingers | About thirty small ones, like a fern | Always exactly two | One single round leaflet
exercise: What does a fallen horse chestnut leaf leave on the twig? | A horseshoe-shaped scar with dots like nails | A sticky brown drop of honeydew that hardens in winter | A tiny conker | A small white catkin
exercise: How can you tell a sweet chestnut tree? | Long toothed leaves and bark that spirals round the trunk | Leaves like a hand and spinning seeds | White bark and triangle leaves | Heart-shaped leaves and sweet-smelling flowers
exercise: Which tree has white bark and small triangular leaves? | Birch | Oak | Lime | Sweet chestnut
exercise: What do people drink from birch trees in Ukraine in spring? | Birch sap | Birch milk | Birch tea made of bark only | Nothing, birch is poisonous
exercise: Why do cars under a lime tree get sticky? | Aphids on the leaves drop sweet honeydew | The tree leaks a sticky glue from its bark in summer | Bees spit honey on them | The lime fruit drips juice
exercise: Is a lime tree related to the lime fruit? | No, they only share a name | Yes, it is the same plant | Yes, lime trees grow small green limes | Only in hot countries
exercise: Where do willows like to grow? | On wet ground, often by water | On dry, rocky hills in the sun | Only in deserts | Only inside forests
exercise: What was made from a chemical like the one in willow bark? | Aspirin | Toothpaste | Chocolate | Soap
exercise: What are willow catkins? | Soft flowers, early food for bees | Tiny furry caterpillars | Baby birds' nests | Seeds wrapped in fur
exercise: What does each ring in a tree stump show? | One year of growth | One month of growth | One branch of the tree | One drought only
exercise: About how much do many trees grow around the trunk each year? | About 2.5 centimetres | About one metre | About one millimetre in ten years | About 25 centimetres
test: «pussy willow — so there's cats in it lol» What are they really? | Soft flower catkins named for feeling like a cat's paw | Fluffy seeds that blow away and land on cats in the spring | Nests made by wild cats | Mice hiding in the branches
test: What would a thin ring tell you about that year? | It was a dry or cold year with little growth | It was a very warm, wet year when the tree grew fast | It was a very good year | The tree was cut that year
test: «my uncle says to count rings you just chop it down, easy» Why is measuring better? | You can guess the age without killing the tree | Trees have no rings until they are cut | Chopping always gives the wrong number | Measuring tells you the exact birthday
test: Count them: a trunk is 2 metres around and grows about 2.5 centimetres a year. About how old might it be? | About 80 years | About 8 years | About 800 years | About 2 years
test: In a cartoon all the park trees are green lollipops, all the same. What can you use to tell real trees apart in winter? | Bark, buds and the shape of the tree | Nothing, without leaves they are all the same | Only the colour of the soil | Only the birds sitting in them
test: «black buds — beech, innit» What is right? | Black buds mean ash; beech has long pointed buds | Yes, beech has black buds, and ash has long pointed ones | Yes, beech has black buds | Black buds mean birch
test: «just roast these conkers off the big tree with hand-shaped leaves» What is right? | That is a horse chestnut; only sweet chestnuts with spiral bark are roasted | Fine, hand-shaped leaves always mean a sweet chestnut, and its nuts roast well | Any chestnut is safe to roast | Conkers taste best roasted
test: Why would a seed spin as it falls? | Spinning slows the fall so the wind carries it further | Spinning helps it drill down into the ground when it lands | Spinning keeps the seed warm | Spinning shakes off insects
test: Why do willow catkins help bees so much? | They flower very early, when there is little other food | They give bees a soft, warm place to sleep through the cold winter | They give bees a place to sleep | Bees eat the willow bark
test: Why might birches be among the first trees to grow on open land? | Their light seeds spread far and they grow well in the open | They can only grow under other trees | They grow from acorns buried by jays | People plant every birch by hand
test: Count them: 4 squirrels play in the oak and 2 pigeons sit in the lime. How many tails and how many legs? | 6 tails and 20 legs | 6 tails and 24 legs | 4 tails and 20 legs | 6 tails and 12 legs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leaf of the horse chestnut? | It is composed of five to seven leaflets arranged like the fingers of a hand | It is a single leaf with a wavy margin | It is small, triangular and borne upon white twigs | It is heart-shaped and secretes honeydew
exam: Identify the principal reason for which vehicles stationed beneath lime trees in summer become sticky. | Aphids feeding upon the foliage excrete a sweet substance designated honeydew | The bark of the lime secretes an adhesive resin | The fruit of the lime releases its juice upon ripening | Bees deposit honey upon objects beneath their foraging sites
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the annual rings of a tree. | Each ring corresponds to one year of growth, its width reflecting the favourability of conditions | Each ring corresponds to one month of growth, so that a tree of one year displays twelve rings | The number of rings bears no relation to the age of the tree | Rings are formed solely in years of drought
exam: Ascertain the relation between the bark of the willow and a medicine in present use. | A chemical in willow bark, formerly used to relieve pain, resembles that from which aspirin was later made | Willow bark is the source of all modern antibiotics | Willow bark is employed in the manufacture of vaccines | No substance of medical interest has been found in willow bark
